      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The supplied evidence directly addresses the comparison. The Abstract states the Croatian work was connected to the beginnings of MT in the USA and USSR and that the Croatian group's cybernetic approach differed from the usual logical approaches of the period. One passage details four USSR approaches (experimental, logical/Steklov, information-theoretic/cybernetic by Lyapunov-Kulagina-Dobrushin, and intermediary-language by Andreev), noting the cybernetic information-theoretic approach was the main role model for the Croatian efforts from 1957. Another passage states the poorly funded Croatian research lagged only a couple of years behind the superpowers, that unlike US/USSR colleagues Laszlo's group lacked an actual computer with results mainly theoretical, and that elsewhere computational linguistics was a top-priority military effort. The evidence also notes the US/UK prototypes (1954-1956) and the Croatian group's lack of citation detail. All comparison targets (US, USSR, Croatian) are covered, so the evidence is sufficient to answer."
      },
